{
  "$meta": {
    "name": "Generic error phrases",
    "description": "The wording ERROR-2 bans, per locale: messages that do not say what failed, why, or what to do. Every app's error-copy guard reads this list (scripts/check-error-copy.mjs, or a vendored copy, or a Dart twin reading BabelGenericErrorPhrases). Brand-neutral.",
    "owner": "design",
    "matching": {
      "normalize": "Unicode NFC; lower case; ’ ‘ ʼ ´ ` become '; “ ” « » become \"; Arabic tashkil (U+064B–U+0652) and tatweel (U+0640) removed; every run of whitespace (including no-break and narrow no-break spaces) becomes one space; leading characters that are neither letters nor digits (emoji, bullets, punctuation) removed; trimmed. Phrases below are stored already normalized.",
      "whole": "The normalized message, with trailing punctuation and spaces removed, IS the phrase. Exempt: a status badge (ERROR-2), which an app lists in its guard's `allow` with the reason. A plain imperative such as \"try again\" / « réessayez » is a button label and is not listed in any locale; its polite message form (\"please try again\", « veuillez réessayer ») is.",
      "opening": "The normalized message starts with the phrase, and the next character is not a letter or digit (a word boundary). No boundary is needed after a Han, Hiragana, Katakana or Hangul character, or after a phrase that ends in punctuation (« impossible d' »).",
      "anywhere": "The phrase appears anywhere in the normalized message, at word boundaries on both sides (the same exemption for Han, Hiragana, Katakana and Hangul).",
      "sourceLiterals": "In source code, only literals that read as prose are checked: they start with an upper-case or non-Latin letter, or contain a space. Wire values ('failed', 'error.generic.internal') and translation keys are not prose.",
      "causeless": "An opening that can carry a cause (\"could not …\", « impossible de … », « échec … », « не удалось … »). It matches only when no cause follows: the message opens with the phrase (as for `opening`), its first clause holds none of the locale's `causeWords`, and every later clause is one of the locale's `filler` sentences or empty. Clauses are split at . ! ? : ; , ( and a spaced dash, and at their CJK and Arabic forms. \"Could not load the tasks. Please try again.\" matches; \"Could not save: the server did not answer within 30 s.\" does not.",
      "filler": "Per locale: clauses that add no cause and no specific next step (\"please try again\", \"sorry\"). Used only by `causeless`; never banned on their own.",
      "causeWords": "Per locale: words that introduce a cause inside the first clause (\"because\", « parce que »). Used only by `causeless`."
    },
    "locales": "en, fr, es and zh for Babel's admin panel and mobile app and for Rendee; pt for Babel's website; ar, de, it, ja, ko and ru for Babel's chatbot. A guard checks a catalogue against its own locale and against en (the source language an untranslated value is left in), and a source literal (whose locale is unknown) against every locale. Every locale lists whole, opening and anywhere; causeless, filler and causeWords may be empty where the language does not open a sentence that way (ja, ko).",
    "provenance": {
      "measuredOn": "2026-10-20",
      "source": "The clear-errors program inventory (ERRORS_PROGRAM_2026-10-20.md): every generic phrase found in the Babel and Rendee catalogues and sources, plus the standard translations of each."
    }
  },
